# PART 6A - ADVANCED: agents as FastMCP + A2A servers, tracing, a JWT-protected API, and two user interfaces
This part builds four lines of the QueryMesh project: (6) every agent exposes a FastMCP server with SSE transport and talks to its peers with the A2A protocol, (7) Logfire span tracing and Langfuse Cloud token tracking, (9) a Telegram bot and a Gradio web UI that stream the answer, and the JWT authentication part of line (11).
You start from the result of Part 5: `python -m querymesh.graph "..."` runs the five agents inside ONE Python process. In this part each agent becomes its own small web server, exactly like microservices in a company.
## BEFORE YOU START: the words of this part
§ Word § Plain meaning
§ server § a program that waits for requests on a port (for example 8101) and answers them
§ MCP (Model Context Protocol) § an open standard for exposing tools to AI programs: "here is a tool called retrieve, it takes query and k" [24]
§ FastMCP § the Python library we use to build MCP servers and clients with a few lines [23]
§ SSE (Server-Sent Events) § a way for a server to keep a connection open and push messages to the client. Our MCP servers use the SSE transport at /mcp/sse
§ A2A (Agent2Agent) § an open protocol for agents to discover each other and hand work to each other (we use spec 1.0 via a2a-sdk 1.2.3) [25]
§ Agent Card § a small JSON file every A2A agent publishes at /.well-known/agent-card.json: its name, what it can do (skills), and where to reach it
§ agent discovery § reading Agent Cards to find out which agent offers which skill, instead of hard-coding it
§ peer delegation § one agent asking another agent for help directly, without going through the supervisor
§ span § one timed step in a trace, e.g. "POST /ask took 14.2 s", inside it "agent call retrieve took 0.8 s"
§ token § a piece of a word; LLMs read and write tokens, and tokens are what LLM usage is measured in
§ JWT (JSON Web Token) § a signed text that says "this is user admin, valid until 15:30". The API checks the signature instead of a password on every call [31]
§ tmux § a terminal program whose windows keep running when you close VS Code or lose the connection

The plan of this part:
6A.1 to 6A.4  agent discovery + the five agent servers, then switch the supervisor to remote mode
6A.5 to 6A.6  Logfire and Langfuse accounts, keys and dashboards
6A.7 to 6A.8  the JWT login and the API, with curl tests and automatic tests
6A.9 to 6A.10 the Gradio web chat and the Telegram bot
## SECTION 6A.0 - What, why, when
**What:** five separate servers (retriever 8101, grader 8102, writer 8103, critic 8104, scout 8105). Each one offers its skill twice: as an MCP tool over SSE (the supervisor calls it) and as an A2A agent with an Agent Card (other agents discover it and delegate to it). In front of them sits the API (8090) with JWT login, and two user interfaces call the API.
**Why:** separate servers can be scaled, restarted and updated one by one (in Part 6B Kubernetes runs each as its own Deployment). Standard protocols (MCP, A2A) mean any MCP- or A2A-speaking program can use your agents, not only your own supervisor.
**When:** split into services when several people or programs use the system, or when one agent needs more machines than another. For a single script, Part 5's local mode is simpler (and stays available with WORKER_MODE=local).
How the pieces talk:
```
Gradio / Telegram --HTTP + JWT--> API :8090 --(cache hit? return)--> supervisor (LangGraph)
supervisor --MCP over SSE--> retriever :8101 | grader :8102 | writer :8103 | critic :8104 | scout :8105
retriever --A2A (peer delegation)--> scout          everyone --spans--> Logfire    LLM calls --tokens--> Langfuse
```
## SECTION 6A.1 - Create querymesh/discovery.py (Agent Cards, discovery, delegation)
WHERE: VS Code Explorer, folder `querymesh`
GOAL: four helpers: download an Agent Card, build the skill phone book for the supervisor, find a peer that offers a skill, and send a task to a peer over A2A.
Step 1. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 2. Click **New File...**.
Step 3. Type `discovery.py` and press Enter.
Step 4. Paste the grey box below.
Step 5. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/discovery.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `from a2a.client import A2ACardResolver, AgentCardResolutionError, ClientConfig, create_client` § the A2A client tools: a card downloader, the error it raises when a card cannot be fetched, client settings, and a factory that builds the right client for an agent
§ `from a2a.helpers import new_text_message` § builds an A2A message from plain text
§ `from a2a.types import Role, SendMessageRequest` § Role.ROLE_USER marks the message as coming from the caller; SendMessageRequest wraps it
§ `async def fetch_card(base_url)` § downloads http://AGENT/.well-known/agent-card.json and turns it into an AgentCard object
§ `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=10)` § wait at most 10 seconds for an agent
§ `async def discover_agents(base_urls)` § agent discovery for the supervisor: for every URL in AGENT_URLS, read the card and note "skill id -> MCP address"
§ `registry[skill.id] = base_url.rstrip("/") + "/mcp/sse"` § e.g. "retrieve" -> "http://localhost:8101/mcp/sse". rstrip removes a trailing slash so we never get //
§ `async def find_peer(base_urls, skill_id)` § used by an agent to find a colleague, e.g. the retriever looking for whoever offers "scout"
§ `except AgentCardResolutionError: continue` § if one agent is down or is not an A2A agent, skip it and keep looking (A2ACardResolver wraps connection errors in this error type)
§ `if any(skill.id == skill_id for skill in card.skills): return base_url` § found the colleague: return its address
§ `async def delegate(base_url, payload)` § peer delegation: send a task to another agent over A2A and wait for the result
§ `create_client(agent=card, client_config=ClientConfig(streaming=False))` § builds a client that talks the protocol written in the card (JSON-RPC at /a2a); no streaming, we wait for the full result
§ `new_text_message(json.dumps(payload), role=Role.ROLE_USER)` § our arguments travel as JSON text inside the A2A message
§ `async for response in client.send_message(request)` § the agent answers with one or more responses; the last one holds the finished task
§ `response.HasField("task") and response.task.artifacts` § A2A 1.0 uses protobuf objects; HasField checks the response contains a task with at least one artifact (a result)
§ `json.loads(response.task.artifacts[0].parts[0].text)` § the artifact text is the JSON result the agent produced
§ `finally: await client.close()` § always close the connection, even after an error

CHECKPOINT: `python -c "import querymesh.discovery"` prints nothing (no error).
If not: an ImportError for a2a means the venv is not active: `source .venv/bin/activate`.
## SECTION 6A.2 - Create querymesh/agents.py (the five FastMCP + A2A servers)
WHERE: VS Code Explorer, folder `querymesh`
GOAL: one file that can start any of the five agents. Each agent server has /healthz, an Agent Card, an A2A endpoint at /a2a and an MCP server with SSE transport at /mcp/sse.
Step 1. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 2. Click **New File...**.
Step 3. Type `agents.py` and press Enter.
Step 4. Paste the grey box below.
Step 5. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/agents.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ the docstring at the top § explains the double exposure: MCP for the supervisor, A2A for peers, and how to start one agent
§ `import uvicorn` § the web server program that runs our app
§ `from a2a.helpers import get_message_text, new_task_from_user_message, new_text_message, new_text_part` § helpers to read the incoming message and to build the task, status message and result
§ `from a2a.server.agent_execution import AgentExecutor, RequestContext` § the base class for "what happens when an A2A request arrives", and the request details
§ `from a2a.server.events import EventQueue` § the queue where our agent puts progress and results for the caller
§ `from a2a.server.request_handlers import DefaultRequestHandler` § standard A2A request handling (tasks, statuses)
§ `from a2a.server.routes import create_agent_card_routes, create_jsonrpc_routes` § web routes for the Agent Card and for the JSON-RPC endpoint
§ `from a2a.server.tasks import InMemoryTaskStore, TaskUpdater` § keeps tasks in memory; TaskUpdater sends artifacts and status changes
§ `from a2a.types import AgentCapabilities, AgentCard, AgentInterface, AgentSkill, TaskState` § the pieces of an Agent Card and the task states
§ `from fastmcp import FastMCP` § the MCP server class
§ `from starlette...` § Starlette is the small web framework that holds everything together (FastAPI is built on it)
§ `PEER_MIN_HITS = 3` § if the retriever finds fewer than 3 chunks, it asks the scout for help
§ `async def retrieve(query, k=8)` § the Retriever's MCP tool. The type hints (str, int) become the tool's input schema automatically; the docstring becomes the tool description
§ `await asyncio.to_thread(skills.retrieve, query, k)` § run the normal search function in a helper thread so the server can serve other requests meanwhile
§ `scout_url = await find_peer(config.AGENT_URLS, "scout")` § agent discovery: read the cards and find who offers "scout"
§ `await delegate(scout_url, {"topic": query, "max_papers": 5})` § A2A peer delegation: the retriever asks the scout directly to fetch 5 fresh papers
§ `hits = await asyncio.to_thread(skills.retrieve, query, k)` § search again, now including the new papers
§ `async def grade / write / critic / scout` § the other four tools: thin async wrappers around the Part 5 skills
§ `AGENTS = {...}` § the table of all agents: name -> port, tool function, one-line description
§ `class SkillExecutor(AgentExecutor)` § what an agent does when an A2A message arrives
§ `task = context.current_task or new_task_from_user_message(context.message)` § continue an existing task, or start a new one from the incoming message
§ `await event_queue.enqueue_event(task)` § tell the caller "task created"
§ `arguments = json.loads(get_message_text(context.message) or "{}")` § the message text is JSON arguments, e.g. {"topic": "...", "max_papers": 5}
§ `result = await self.tool(**arguments)` § run the same tool the MCP server offers
§ `updater.add_artifact(parts=[new_text_part(text=json.dumps(result), media_type="application/json")])` § attach the result to the task as JSON text
§ `updater.update_status(state=TaskState.TASK_STATE_COMPLETED, ...)` § mark the task finished
§ `async def cancel(...): raise NotImplementedError` § cancelling is not supported; our tasks are short
§ `def build_app(name)` § builds the complete web app for one agent
§ `skill_id = spec["tool"].__name__` § the tool's function name ("retrieve", "grade", ...) becomes both the MCP tool name and the A2A skill id
§ `public_url = os.getenv("PUBLIC_URL", f"http://localhost:{spec['port']}")` § the address written into the Agent Card. On the VM it is localhost; in Docker/Kubernetes it is the service name (Part 6B sets PUBLIC_URL)
§ `mcp = FastMCP(f"querymesh-{name}")` and `mcp.tool(spec["tool"])` § create the MCP server and register the tool
§ `mcp.http_app(transport="sse", path="/sse")` § turn the MCP server into a web app that uses the SSE transport
§ `card = AgentCard(...)` § the agent's business card: name, description, version, input/output types (JSON), capabilities (no streaming), the JSON-RPC address /a2a with protocol version 1.0, and one skill
§ `DefaultRequestHandler(agent_executor=SkillExecutor(spec["tool"]), task_store=InMemoryTaskStore(), agent_card=card)` § wires the executor, the task memory and the card together
§ `Route("/healthz", healthz)` § a tiny "I am alive" page for Docker and Kubernetes health checks
§ `*create_agent_card_routes(card)` § serves the card at /.well-known/agent-card.json
§ `*create_jsonrpc_routes(handler, "/a2a")` § the A2A endpoint
§ `Mount("/mcp", app=mcp_app)` § the MCP app lives under /mcp, so its SSE endpoint is /mcp/sse (and messages go to /mcp/messages/)
§ `Starlette(routes=routes, lifespan=mcp_app.lifespan)` § one app with all routes; the MCP app's start-up/shut-down hooks must be passed on, or MCP sessions do not start
§ `agent_name = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "retriever"` § the first word after the command chooses the agent
§ `observability.setup(f"querymesh-{agent_name}")` § each agent appears in Logfire under its own service name
§ `port = int(os.getenv("PORT", AGENTS[agent_name]["port"]))` § the port from the table, unless PORT is set
§ `uvicorn.run(build_app(agent_name), host="0.0.0.0", port=port)` § start the server on all network interfaces of the VM (the Hyperstack firewall still blocks these ports from the internet)

CHECKPOINT: `python -c "from querymesh.agents import build_app; build_app('grader'); print('ok')"` prints ok.
If not: compare the file with the grey box; a typo in an import is the usual cause.
## SECTION 6A.3 - Start the five agents and check them (Agent Card, MCP, A2A)
WHERE: VS Code Terminal, folder `~/querymesh`, venv active
GOAL: five agent servers running in one tmux session (they keep running when VS Code disconnects), each answering on its Agent Card, its MCP tool and A2A.
Step 1. Start the tmux session with the first agent (retriever):
```
tmux new-session -d -s agents -n retriever "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.agents retriever; bash"
```
Step 2. Add the grader in a second window:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n grader "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.agents grader; bash"
```
Step 3. Add the writer:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n writer "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.agents writer; bash"
```
Step 4. Add the critic:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n critic "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.agents critic; bash"
```
Step 5. Add the scout:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n scout "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.agents scout; bash"
```
§ Piece § What it does
§ `tmux new-session -d -s agents -n retriever "..."` § create a session called agents, detached (-d, in the background), with a first window called retriever that runs the command
§ `tmux new-window -t agents -n grader "..."` § add a window called grader to the session agents
§ `; bash` at the end § if the agent crashes, the window stays open with a shell, so you can read the error
Step 6. Wait 5 seconds, then check all five health pages:
```
for p in 8101 8102 8103 8104 8105; do curl -s localhost:$p/healthz; echo; done
```
You should see:
```
{"status":"ok","agent":"retriever"}
{"status":"ok","agent":"grader"}
{"status":"ok","agent":"writer"}
{"status":"ok","agent":"critic"}
{"status":"ok","agent":"scout"}
```
Step 7. Read the retriever's Agent Card:
```
curl -s localhost:8101/.well-known/agent-card.json | jq .
```
You should see (shortened):
```
{
  "name": "querymesh-retriever",
  "description": "Finds arXiv passages with hybrid search",
  "supportedInterfaces": [{"url": "http://localhost:8101/a2a", "protocolBinding": "JSONRPC", "protocolVersion": "1.0"}],
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "capabilities": {"streaming": false},
  "defaultInputModes": ["application/json"],
  "skills": [{"id": "retrieve", "name": "retrieve", "description": "Finds arXiv passages with hybrid search", "tags": ["querymesh"]}]
}
```
Step 8. Call the retriever's MCP tool over SSE with a small FastMCP client (paste the whole block at once):
```
python - <<'EOF'
import asyncio
from fastmcp import Client

async def main():
    async with Client("http://localhost:8101/mcp/sse") as mcp:
        print([tool.name for tool in await mcp.list_tools()])
        result = await mcp.call_tool("retrieve", {"query": "hybrid search", "k": 3})
        print([chunk["paper_id"] for chunk in result.data])

asyncio.run(main())
EOF
```
You should see: ['retrieve'] and then a list of 3 arXiv ids.
§ Piece § What it does
§ `python - <<'EOF' ... EOF` § runs the Python lines between the two EOF markers without creating a file
§ `Client("http://localhost:8101/mcp/sse")` § FastMCP chooses the SSE transport because the URL ends in /sse
§ `list_tools()` § asks the server which tools it offers (MCP discovery)
§ `call_tool("retrieve", {...})` § calls the tool; `result.data` is the decoded return value (a list of chunks)
Step 9. Test A2A: ask the scout agent directly for two fresh papers (peer delegation by hand):
```
python -c "import asyncio; from querymesh.discovery import delegate; print(asyncio.run(delegate('http://localhost:8105', {'topic': 'speculative decoding', 'max_papers': 2})))"
```
You should see: {'added': [...]} with 0 to 2 arXiv ids.
Step 10. Test agent discovery: build the supervisor's phone book from the cards:
```
python -c "import asyncio; from querymesh import config; from querymesh.discovery import discover_agents; print(asyncio.run(discover_agents(config.AGENT_URLS)))"
```
You should see: {'retrieve': 'http://localhost:8101/mcp/sse', 'grade': 'http://localhost:8102/mcp/sse', 'write': 'http://localhost:8103/mcp/sse', 'critic': 'http://localhost:8104/mcp/sse', 'scout': 'http://localhost:8105/mcp/sse'}
Step 11. To look at an agent's own log, attach to tmux:
```
tmux attach -t agents
```
Step 12. Switch windows with Ctrl+B then a number (0 = retriever ... 4 = scout). You see one "POST /a2a" or "GET /mcp/sse" line per call.
Step 13. Leave tmux without stopping the agents: press Ctrl+B, release, then press D.
§ If you see... § It means... § Do this next
§ curl: (7) Failed to connect § that agent is not running § `tmux attach -t agents`, open its window, read the error (often a missing .env value)
§ Address already in use § an old agent still holds the port § `tmux kill-session -t agents`, then repeat Steps 1-5
§ ModuleNotFoundError: querymesh § the window did not start in ~/querymesh § check the command in Steps 1-5 starts with cd ~/querymesh
§ Step 10 raises AgentCardResolutionError § one of the five agents is down § check Step 6, start the missing one
CHECKPOINT: Step 6 shows five ok lines, Step 8 prints 3 ids, Step 10 prints all five skills.
If not: use the table above.
## SECTION 6A.4 - Switch the supervisor to remote mode (agents over MCP, peers over A2A)
WHERE: VS Code, file `.env`, then the Terminal
GOAL: the same graph as Part 5, but every agent call now travels over MCP/SSE to the agent servers.
Step 1. Switch WORKER_MODE to remote in .env:
```
sed -i 's/^WORKER_MODE=.*/WORKER_MODE=remote/' .env
```
Step 2. Check it:
```
grep -E '^(WORKER_MODE|AGENT_URLS)=' .env
```
You should see: WORKER_MODE=remote and AGENT_URLS=http://localhost:8101,...,http://localhost:8105
Step 3. Run the team again:
```
python -m querymesh.graph "How does reciprocal rank fusion merge BM25 and vector results?"
```
You should see: the same kind of lines as in Section 5.5 (retriever, grader, writer, critic, then the answer). When we tested this exact setup with five agent servers, it printed:
```
  .. retriever: 5 chunks (attempt 1)
  .. grader: 5 of 5 chunks are relevant
  .. writer: answer drafted (attempt 1)
  .. critic: grounded=True
```
Step 4. Run `tmux attach -t agents` and look at the retriever window (Ctrl+B 0).
You should see: "GET /mcp/sse" and "POST /mcp/messages/..." lines: the supervisor reached the retriever over MCP with SSE.
Step 5. Press Ctrl+B then D to leave tmux.
How peer delegation shows up: when the retriever finds fewer than 3 chunks, it finds the scout through the Agent Cards and sends it a task over A2A, then searches again. In our test the scout received the A2A task from the retriever, without the supervisor asking for it. You see it as a "POST /a2a" line in the scout window (Ctrl+B 4).
Watch out: in remote mode, if any one agent is down, the first call raises AgentCardResolutionError (discovery needs all five cards) and the question fails loudly. That is on purpose: you notice at once. Start the missing agent (Section 6A.3) and ask again.
Watch out: the agents read .env only when they start. After you change .env, restart them: `tmux kill-session -t agents` and repeat Section 6A.3 Steps 1-5.
CHECKPOINT: Step 3 prints an answer and the retriever window shows MCP lines.
If not: set WORKER_MODE=local to confirm the graph itself works, then fix the agents with the table in Section 6A.3.
## SECTION 6A.5 - Logfire: see every span (free)
WHERE: web browser on your laptop, then VS Code
GOAL: a Logfire project and a write token in .env, so every request, agent call and HTTP call appears as a timed span on the website [26].
Step 1. Open https://logfire.pydantic.dev
Step 2. Click **Sign up** and sign in with GitHub (or Google).
Step 3. When asked, choose a region (US or EU; pick the one nearer to you) and a username.
Step 4. Click **New project**.
Step 5. Type the project name `querymesh` and click **Create**.
Step 6. In the project, click the gear icon **Settings**.
Step 7. Click **Write tokens**.
Step 8. Click **New write token**.
Step 9. Click the copy icon next to the token (it starts with pylf_ and is shown only once).
Step 10. In VS Code open `.env` and paste it after `LOGFIRE_TOKEN=`:
```
LOGFIRE_TOKEN=pylf_v1_us_YOUR_TOKEN
```
Step 11. Press Ctrl+S.
Step 12. Restart the agents so they read the token:
```
tmux kill-session -t agents
```
Step 13. Repeat Section 6A.3 Steps 1-5 (start the five agents again).
Step 14. Ask one question:
```
python -m querymesh.graph "What is late interaction retrieval?"
```
Step 15. In the Logfire website click **Live** (the live view).
You should see: rows for the services querymesh-retriever, querymesh-grader, querymesh-writer, querymesh-critic, spans named "agent call retrieve", "agent call grade" ..., and HTTP spans for the calls to api.jina.ai and to localhost:8000 (vLLM). Click a row to see its duration.
Step 16. Click the "agent call write" span.
You should see: the time the writer took (usually the longest, several seconds) and the attributes name=write, mode=remote.
Watch out: the free Personal plan includes 10 million spans/logs/metrics per month [26]; QueryMesh uses a few dozen spans per question, so you will not reach it while learning.
CHECKPOINT: Logfire Live shows spans from at least four querymesh services.
If not: no data at all means LOGFIRE_TOKEN was not loaded: check the line in .env has no spaces, and restart the agents.
## SECTION 6A.6 - Langfuse Cloud: see tokens and prompts (free)
WHERE: web browser on your laptop, then VS Code
GOAL: every LLM call (prompt, answer, input and output tokens, time) recorded in Langfuse Cloud [27].
Step 1. Open https://cloud.langfuse.com (EU region; for the US region use https://us.cloud.langfuse.com).
Step 2. Click **Sign up** and create an account (GitHub, Google or e-mail).
Step 3. Click **New Organization**, type `querymesh`, click **Create**.
Step 4. Click **New Project**, type `querymesh`, click **Create**.
Step 5. Open **Settings** (left menu) and click **API Keys**.
Step 6. Click **Create new API keys**.
Step 7. Copy the **Public Key** (starts with pk-lf-) and the **Secret Key** (starts with sk-lf-; shown only once).
Step 8. In VS Code open `.env` and fill the three Langfuse lines:
```
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-YOUR_PUBLIC_KEY
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-YOUR_SECRET_KEY
LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://cloud.langfuse.com
```
Step 9. Press Ctrl+S.
Step 10. Restart the agents (they make the LLM calls in remote mode):
```
tmux kill-session -t agents
```
Step 11. Repeat Section 6A.3 Steps 1-5.
Step 12. Ask one question:
```
python -m querymesh.graph "What problems does lost-in-the-middle cause for RAG?"
```
Step 13. In Langfuse click **Tracing** (or **Traces**) in the left menu.
You should see: several new traces named ChatOpenAI: one from the grader, one from the writer, one from the critic (more if the loop retried).
Step 14. Click one trace, then the generation inside it.
You should see: the full prompt (with the numbered passages), the model's answer, the model name qwen3-4b, and the usage: input tokens, output tokens, total.
Step 15. (Optional) To see a cost, give your model a price: **Settings > Models > Add model definition**:
§ Field § Value § Why
§ Model name § qwen3-4b § must match the name in the traces
§ Match pattern § (?i)^qwen3-4b$ § which traces this price applies to
§ Input / output price § your GPU cost per token, e.g. 0.0000001 § self-hosted models have no public price; this lets you compare runs
Watch out: the free Hobby plan gives 50,000 units per month (a unit = a trace, an observation or a score) and keeps data 30 days [27]. One question creates about 5-10 units, so about 5,000 questions per month.
CHECKPOINT: Langfuse shows ChatOpenAI traces with token counts for your last question.
If not: no traces = keys not loaded (restart the agents) or wrong region (keys from cloud.langfuse.com do not work on us.cloud.langfuse.com).
## SECTION 6A.7 - Create auth.py and api.py, then test the API with curl
WHERE: VS Code Explorer, folder `querymesh`, then the Terminal
GOAL: a web API with a login (POST /token gives a JWT), a protected POST /ask, and POST /ask/stream that streams the agents' progress as Server-Sent Events, with the cache in front.
Step 1. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 2. Click **New File...**.
Step 3. Type `auth.py` and press Enter.
Step 4. Paste the grey box below.
Step 5. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/auth.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `import jwt` § PyJWT, the library that signs and checks JSON Web Tokens
§ `from fastapi.security import OAuth2PasswordBearer` § tells FastAPI "callers send Authorization: Bearer TOKEN"; it also adds a login button to the /docs page
§ `oauth2_scheme = OAuth2PasswordBearer(tokenUrl="token")` § the token is obtained at POST /token
§ `def create_token(username)` § makes a new JWT for this user
§ `claims = {"sub": username, "iat": now, "exp": now + timedelta(minutes=config.JWT_MINUTES)}` § the token says: subject (who), issued at (when), expires (60 minutes later)
§ `jwt.encode(claims, config.JWT_SECRET, algorithm="HS256")` § signs the claims with your secret. Anyone can READ a JWT, but nobody can CHANGE it without the secret
§ `def check_password(username, password)` § compares with API_USERNAME and API_PASSWORD from .env (one admin user is enough for this project)
§ `def current_user(token: str = Depends(oauth2_scheme))` § FastAPI runs this before every protected endpoint and gives it the token from the header
§ `jwt.decode(token, config.JWT_SECRET, algorithms=["HS256"])` § checks the signature and the expiry time; fails if either is wrong
§ `except jwt.PyJWTError: raise HTTPException(401, ...)` § bad or expired token = 401 Unauthorized
§ `return claims["sub"]` § the endpoint receives the user name

Step 6. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 7. Click **New File...**.
Step 8. Type `api.py` and press Enter.
Step 9. Paste the grey box below.
Step 10. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/api.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `app = FastAPI(title="QueryMesh API", version="1.0.0")` § the web application; FastAPI also builds interactive docs at /docs
§ `observability.setup("querymesh-api", app)` § Logfire spans for every request to the API
§ `class Question(BaseModel): question: str = Field(min_length=3, max_length=500)` § the request body must be JSON like {"question": "..."}, 3 to 500 characters; anything else gets 422
§ `@app.get("/healthz")` § "I am alive" for Docker, Kubernetes and the load balancer
§ `@app.post("/token")` with `OAuth2PasswordRequestForm` § login: reads username and password from a form, returns {"access_token": "...", "token_type": "bearer"}
§ `raise HTTPException(401, "wrong username or password")` § wrong login = 401
§ `@app.post("/ask")` with `user: str = Depends(current_user)` § protected: FastAPI checks the JWT first; no or bad token = 401 before any agent runs
§ `cached = cache.get(body.question)` / `return {**cached, "cached": True}` § cache hit: answer in milliseconds, no GPU work
§ `async for kind, value in stream_answer(body.question)` § run the supervisor graph from Part 5 and keep the final result
§ `cache.put(body.question, final)` § store the answer for 24 hours
§ `@app.post("/ask/stream")` § the same, but streamed: the caller sees each agent step as it happens
§ `async def events():` § a generator that produces Server-Sent Events
§ `yield f"event: final\ndata: {json.dumps(...)}\n\n"` § SSE format: a line "event: NAME", a line "data: JSON", then an empty line ends the event
§ `StreamingResponse(events(), media_type="text/event-stream")` § sends the events one by one over the open connection

Step 11. Start the API in a new tmux window of the agents session:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n api "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/uvicorn querymesh.api:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8090; bash"
```
Step 12. Check it is alive:
```
curl -s localhost:8090/healthz
```
You should see: {"status":"ok"}
Step 13. Try /ask WITHOUT a token:
```
curl -s -X POST localhost:8090/ask -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"question": "What is RRF?"}'
```
You should see: {"detail":"Not authenticated"} (HTTP 401: the JWT protection works)
Step 14. Log in and keep the token in a shell variable:
```
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST localhost:8090/token -d "username=admin&password=$(grep ^API_PASSWORD .env | cut -d= -f2)" | jq -r .access_token)
```
Step 15. Look at the token:
```
echo $TOKEN | cut -c1-40
```
You should see: text starting with eyJ (every JWT starts like this).
Step 16. Ask with the token:
```
curl -s -X POST localhost:8090/ask -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"question": "How does reciprocal rank fusion work?"}' | jq '{cached, grounded, answer: .answer[0:120]}'
```
You should see (after 10-40 seconds): "cached": false, "grounded": true, and the start of the answer.
Step 17. Ask the SAME question again and time it:
```
time curl -s -X POST localhost:8090/ask -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"question": "how does reciprocal  rank fusion work?"}' | jq .cached
```
You should see: true, and real time well under 1 second: a cache hit from Upstash (the different spaces and capitals do not matter: normalisation + SHA-256).
Step 18. Watch the streaming endpoint (-N = do not buffer):
```
curl -N -s -X POST localhost:8090/ask/stream -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"question": "What is ColBERT late interaction?"}'
```
You should see: lines appearing one after another:
```
event: step
data: "retriever: 8 chunks (attempt 1)"

event: step
data: "grader: 3 of 8 chunks are relevant"
...
event: final
data: {"answer": "...", "citations": [...], "grounded": true, "cached": false}
```
Step 19. (Optional) Open the interactive docs: in VS Code click the **PORTS** tab, click **Forward a Port**, type 8090, press Enter, then open http://localhost:8090/docs on your laptop.
§ If you see... § It means... § Do this next
§ {"detail":"wrong username or password"} § API_PASSWORD in .env differs from what you typed § use the grep command exactly as in Step 14
§ {"detail":"invalid or expired token"} § the token is older than 60 minutes or JWT_SECRET changed § repeat Step 14
§ 422 Unprocessable Entity § the JSON body is wrong (missing "question" or shorter than 3 characters) § copy the -d part exactly
§ "cached": false on the second call § the cache is off § check REDIS_URL (Section 5.8) and restart the api window
CHECKPOINT: Step 13 gives Not authenticated, Step 16 gives an answer, Step 17 gives true.
If not: use the table above.
## SECTION 6A.8 - Add the API and ingestion tests, run all tests
WHERE: VS Code Explorer, folder `tests`, then the Terminal
GOAL: automatic tests for the login, the protected endpoints, streaming, the cache key, the chunker and the arXiv query builder.
Step 1. Check that `tests/__init__.py` exists (you created it, empty, in Section 5.7). If it is missing: right-click `tests` > **New File...** > type `__init__.py` > Enter > Ctrl+S.
Step 2. Right-click the folder `tests`.
Step 3. Click **New File...**.
Step 4. Type `test_api.py` and press Enter.
Step 5. Paste the grey box below.
Step 6. Press Ctrl+S.
@code tests/test_api.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `from fastapi.testclient import TestClient` § calls the API in memory, without starting a server
§ `from tests.test_graph import fake_skills` § re-uses the fake agents from Part 5, so no GPU is needed
§ `def token()` § logs in with admin/admin (the defaults when no .env is present, as in GitHub Actions) and returns the JWT
§ `test_health` § /healthz answers {"status": "ok"}
§ `test_wrong_password` § a wrong password gives 401
§ `test_ask_needs_token` § /ask without a token gives 401
§ `test_ask_and_stream` § with fake agents and the cache switched off: /ask returns grounded=True and cached=False; /ask/stream contains step and final events
§ `grounded_sequence=(True, True)` § two critic verdicts, because the test asks twice (once /ask, once /ask/stream)
§ `monkeypatch.setattr(cache, "get", lambda q: None)` § the cache always misses during the test
§ `test_cache_key_ignores_case_and_spaces` § the same question written differently gives the same SHA-256 key; a different question gives a different key

Step 7. Right-click the folder `tests`.
Step 8. Click **New File...**.
Step 9. Type `test_ingest.py` and press Enter.
Step 10. Paste the grey box below.
Step 11. Press Ctrl+S.
@code tests/test_ingest.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `markdown = "# Title..." + "method " * 400 + "## References..."` § a fake paper: a title, a long Method section and a reference list
§ `assert all(len(c) <= CHUNK_CHARS for c in chunks)` § no chunk is longer than 1,500 characters
§ `chunks[0].startswith("# Title")` § the first chunk starts at the first heading
§ `not any("[1] x" in c for c in chunks)` § the reference list was removed
§ `test_topic_to_arxiv_query` § "What is reciprocal rank fusion?" becomes the arXiv query all:reciprocal AND all:rank AND all:fusion

Step 12. Run all tests:
```
pytest -q
```
You should see: `10 passed` (this is the exact result we got when we ran these tests).
Watch out: the tests must not need your .env. GitHub Actions in Part 6B runs them without secrets; that is why they use fakes and the default admin/admin login.
CHECKPOINT: pytest prints 10 passed.
If not: "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tests'" means tests/__init__.py is missing (Step 1).
## SECTION 6A.9 - Create the Gradio web chat (streams each agent step)
WHERE: VS Code Explorer, folder `querymesh`, then the Terminal, then your laptop's browser
GOAL: a web page where you type a question and watch the agents work live, then read the cited answer.
Step 1. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 2. Click **New File...**.
Step 3. Type `api_client.py` and press Enter.
Step 4. Paste the grey box below.
Step 5. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/api_client.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `def get_token()` § logs in to the API with API_USERNAME and API_PASSWORD and returns the JWT. Both user interfaces use it
§ `async def stream_question(question, token)` § calls /ask/stream and turns the SSE lines back into ("step", text) and ("final", dict) pairs
§ `httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=600)` § allow up to 10 minutes for a long agent run
§ `http.stream("POST", ...)` § keep the connection open and read the answer piece by piece
§ `response.raise_for_status()` § stop with a clear error on 401 or 500
§ `if line.startswith("event: ")` § remember the event name (step or final)
§ `elif line.startswith("data: "): yield event, json.loads(...)` § the data line holds the JSON; hand it to the caller together with the event name
§ `def format_final(final)` § turns the final result into readable text: the answer, then "Sources:" with one line per citation, and "(from cache)" when it was a cache hit

Step 6. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 7. Click **New File...**.
Step 8. Type `ui_gradio.py` and press Enter.
Step 9. Paste the grey box below.
Step 10. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/ui_gradio.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `import gradio as gr` § Gradio builds web interfaces for Python functions [29]
§ `async def respond(message, history)` § Gradio calls this for every chat message. history = earlier messages (we do not need them)
§ `token = get_token()` § log in to our API
§ `progress += f"- {value}\n"` and `yield f"Working...\n{progress}"` § each yield replaces the bot's reply on the page, so the user sees the list of steps grow
§ `yield f"{format_final(value)}\n\nAgent steps:\n{progress}"` § the last yield shows the answer, the sources and the steps
§ `gr.ChatInterface(fn=respond, title=..., description=..., examples=[...])` § a ready-made chat page; because respond is a generator, Gradio streams it automatically
§ `demo.launch(server_name="0.0.0.0", server_port=7860)` § serve the page on port 7860 on all interfaces of the VM

Step 11. Start the web chat in a new tmux window:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n gradio "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.ui_gradio; bash"
```
Step 12. In VS Code click the **PORTS** tab (next to TERMINAL).
Step 13. Click **Forward a Port**.
Step 14. Type 7860 and press Enter.
Step 15. On your laptop open http://localhost:7860
You should see: a page titled QueryMesh with a chat box and two example questions.
Step 16. Click the first example question.
You should see: "Working..." and a growing list (- retriever: ..., - grader: ..., ...), then the answer with Sources and Agent steps.
Step 17. Click the same example again.
You should see: the answer appears almost at once with "Sources (from cache):".
Watch out: the web page itself has no login in this project; anyone who can reach port 7860 can ask questions. That is why we reach it only through VS Code port forwarding (the Hyperstack firewall keeps 7860 closed). In Part 6B it sits behind the load balancer; Part 7 lists how to add a login.
CHECKPOINT: the page shows the steps live and then a cited answer.
If not: "Connection refused" in the page means the API window is not running (Section 6A.7 Step 11).
## SECTION 6A.10 - Create the Telegram bot (edits its reply live)
WHERE: the Telegram app (phone or desktop), VS Code, the Terminal
GOAL: a Telegram bot: you send a question, it replies "Working..." and edits that message as the agents work, then shows the cited answer.
Step 1. Open Telegram and search for `@BotFather` (the official one has a blue check mark).
Step 2. Tap **Start** (or send /start).
Step 3. Send:
```
/newbot
```
Step 4. When BotFather asks for a name, send a display name, for example:
```
QueryMesh Research
```
Step 5. When it asks for a username, send a unique name that ends in bot:
```
querymesh_YOURNAME_bot
```
§ Placeholder § Replace with § Example
§ YOURNAME § your name or initials, letters/digits/underscore only § querymesh_karthik_bot
You should see: "Done! Congratulations on your new bot..." and a line "Use this token to access the HTTP API:" followed by a token like 123456789:AAH...
Step 6. Copy the token.
Step 7. In VS Code open `.env` and paste it after `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=`:
```
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:AAH_YOUR_TOKEN
```
Step 8. Press Ctrl+S.
Step 9. Right-click the folder `querymesh`.
Step 10. Click **New File...**.
Step 11. Type `ui_telegram.py` and press Enter.
Step 12. Paste the grey box below.
Step 13. Press Ctrl+S.
@code querymesh/ui_telegram.py
§ Line(s) § What it does (plain English)
§ `from telegram.ext import ApplicationBuilder, CommandHandler, ContextTypes, MessageHandler, filters` § python-telegram-bot: the bot application and the handlers that react to commands and messages [30]
§ `async def start(update, context)` § answers the /start command with a welcome text
§ `async def answer(update, context)` § runs for every normal text message
§ `reply = await update.message.reply_text("Working...")` § sends a first reply and keeps it, so we can edit it
§ `await reply.edit_text(f"Working...\n{progress}")` § edits the same message after each agent step: the "streaming" effect in Telegram
§ `format_final(value)[:4000]` § Telegram messages may have at most 4,096 characters
§ `if not config.TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN: raise SystemExit(...)` § a clear message instead of a confusing error when the token is missing
§ `ApplicationBuilder().token(...).build()` § creates the bot with your token
§ `CommandHandler("start", start)` § /start goes to start()
§ `MessageHandler(filters.TEXT & ~filters.COMMAND, answer)` § any text that is not a command goes to answer()
§ `app.run_polling()` § ask Telegram's servers for new messages again and again. No open port and no public address are needed

Step 14. Start the bot in a new tmux window:
```
tmux new-window -t agents -n telegram "cd ~/querymesh && .venv/bin/python -m querymesh.ui_telegram; bash"
```
Step 15. In Telegram, open your bot: search for querymesh_YOURNAME_bot (or tap the t.me link BotFather sent).
Step 16. Tap **Start**.
You should see: "Hi! I am QueryMesh. Send me a question about arXiv research."
Step 17. Send a question, for example:
```
What are recent methods to reduce hallucination in RAG?
```
You should see: "Working..." that grows with - retriever ..., - grader ..., and is finally replaced by the answer with Sources.
§ If you see... § It means... § Do this next
§ the bot does not answer at all § the telegram window crashed § `tmux attach -t agents`, window telegram, read the error
§ telegram.error.InvalidToken § the token in .env is wrong or has spaces § copy it again from BotFather (send /mybots > your bot > API Token)
§ telegram.error.Conflict § the same bot runs twice (another window or computer) § stop the other copy; only one program may poll a bot
§ Message is not modified § two identical edits in a row § harmless; the next step changes the text
Watch out: anyone who finds your bot can use it. For a private bot, tell BotFather /setjoingroups Disable, and in Part 7 add a check of the Telegram user id.
CHECKPOINT: the bot answers a question with a cited answer.
If not: use the table above.
## SECTION 6A.11 - Common mistake at this level
**Mistake:** changing .env and expecting the running servers to notice. Every process (five agents, API, Gradio, Telegram) reads .env once, at start. The usual symptom: "I added my Logfire token but see no spans" or "the API still uses local mode".
**How to spot it:** `tmux attach -t agents` and look at when each window started; compare with when you saved .env.
**Fix:** restart everything in one go:
```
tmux kill-session -t agents
```
Then repeat Section 6A.3 Steps 1-5, Section 6A.7 Step 11, Section 6A.9 Step 11 and Section 6A.10 Step 14. Part 6B replaces these manual windows with docker compose, which restarts all services with one command.
**Second mistake:** opening ports 8090 or 7860 in the Hyperstack firewall "to make it work". You do not need to: VS Code port forwarding is private and encrypted. Opened ports expose an API and a chat page to the whole internet.
